cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "One Shot, Twenty-One Balls: A Computer-Assisted Proof of a Single-Stroke Total Clearance in an Idealized Model of Snooker"
abstract: >-
  Snooker folklore holds that no single stroke can pocket all twenty-one
  object balls. In an explicit model of billiard dynamics we exhibit a
  configuration and a cue stroke that pocket all twenty-one, and prove in
  interval arithmetic that every stroke in a box around it does too, so
  the set of successful strokes has positive Lebesgue measure. The
  successful strokes form a ribbon: every speed from 9.3213 to 12 m/s
  works, while the window of directions is 9.4813e-43 radians wide, both
  figures certified. The lower edge in speed is located exactly, where the
  slowest ball comes to rest at the lip of its pocket. The strokes with a
  given list of events form a semialgebraic set. For the regulation
  opening position we state the corresponding conjecture, and show that a
  finite certified search would settle it positively. The repository also
  holds the Monte Carlo experiments of the companion expository article.
type: software
